KEYWORDS: molybdenum-based compounds, metal-organic frameworks, one-dimensional, carbon coating, lithium-ion batteries
Lithium-ion batteries as the currently predominant energy storage devices have been widely investigated in recent years because of their many advantages like high energy density, long lifespan and environmental benignity.1-3 Although graphite is the most common anode material, its low theoretical capacity $(372\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}})$ ) largely impedes the development of high-performance LIBs. Therefore, numerous efforts have been made to searching for alternative anode materials.4-7 Among them, molybdenum (Mo)-based compounds have recently attracted great attentions, especially since molybdenum oxides or carbides are proved to be good candidates as anode materials for LIBs due to their relatively high theoretical capacity and excellent electric conductivity.8-10
As a kind of typical transition metal oxides (TMOs), molybdenum dioxide $(\mathrm{MoO}_{2})$ ) has been proposed to be a promising anode material based on its attractive features like high theoretical capacity $\left(838\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\right)$ ), low electrical resistivity and natural abundance.11 However, bulk $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ usually suffers from dramatic volume change and sluggish kinetics during the charge-discharge process, which always results in rapid capacity decay and impedes its practical application. Meanwhile, for transition metal carbides (TMCs), although they have been broadly applied in fields of catalysis and electronics due to their intriguing chemical and physical properties,13,14 the possibility of using TMCs as promising electrodes for LIBs has been investigated both experimentally and theoretically since Gogotsi et al. firstly proved the electrochemical behavior between ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ and exfoliated $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ in 2012.15-17 It was found that the intercalation and deintercalation of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ could occur on these TMCs, indicating their promising lithium storage ability.18 Among various metal carbides studied so far, molybdenum carbide $(\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C})$ has got particular attention due to its high theoretical capacity $(600{-}1000~\mathrm{\mAh~\g^{-})}$ and excellent electric conductivity $(\sim1.02\times10^{2}$ S $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ).19,20 Besides, $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ has a minimum lithium ions diffusion barrier about 0.035 eV according to the previously reported theoretical calculation,21 thus showing great potential for LIBs. Unfortunately, experiments showed that bulk $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ could not react with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ sufficiently due to its undesirable structural features,22 thereby displaying a very low capacity. To solve the above-mentioned problems, the rational structural design of Mo-based compounds at the nanoscale is a popular strategy and various nanostructures have been produced so far, including nanoparticles,23 nanowires,24 nanotubes,25 nanosheets,26 and complex assemblies.27 Profiting from the optimized diffusion path for ions and electrons, these nanostructures were believed to transport ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ to active centers of electrodes efficiently, thus facilitating the inactive lithiation process and improving specific capacities due to a transformation from the insertion to conversion mechanism.28-30 However, it has always been a hot issue that optimizing the desirable nanostructure in a more facile, controllable and low-cost way.to enhance the lithium storage.
Furthermore, surface modification with highly conductive carbon is another common strategy to enhance electrochemical performances of Mo-based compounds,31 as it can effectively improve the conductivity or restrain the volume change during cycles so that keeps the integral structure maintaining stable.32 In recent years, porous carbons or metal oxide/carbon composites derived from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have sprung up and attracted much interest in many fields by virtue of their relatively high surface area, tunable porosity and well-organized crystal structure.33,34 As a subclass of MOFs, it has been reported that zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) can be directly converted to carbon framework with homogeneous transition metal/metal oxides and nitrogen dopings.35-37 Among them, Zn-based ZIF-8 is easily transformed to N-doped carbon with a high surface area and uniform nitrogen distribution after pyrolysis, although it shows an obvious amorphous feature, which possesses less favorable electronic conductivity. In contrast, the carbon-based material derived from Co-based ZIF-67 can have a high degree of graphitization with the catalytic $\mathrm{Co-N_{x}}$ moiety, although its surface area and nitrogen content are decreased.38,39 Thus, it can to be a good choice to introduce bimetallic composites of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 in a proper ratio as the carbon precursor to combine with Mo-based compounds.
Inspired by above-mentioned strategies, herein, we have successfully developed a metal-organic frameworks mediated method to prepare a series of one-dimensional Mo-based/carbon composites with distinct nanostructures, including the core-shell and hollow structures. To start from the well-designed $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods, a layer of bimetallic zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (BMZIFs) is then well achieved to be in situ grown on surfaces of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ controllably and uniformly, forming the ideal precursor with an obvious core-shell structure. Subsequently, during the carbonization process, this strategy shows the advantage of one-step reaction, where the formation of the outer carbon layer derived from BMZIFs as well as reactions between $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ and carbon to form $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ can be occurred simultaneously, maintaining the structural stability of pyrolytic products. As expected, when evaluated as anode materials for LIBs, all products show enhanced electrochemical performances owing to their structure merits. Besides, more detailed analyses about structural compositions and reaction mechanisms are discussed in following sections of this paper.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The typical preparation procedure is schematically illustrated in Figure 1. As the precursor of Mo-based compounds, $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods were firstly synthesized. Then after a layer of Zn, Co-BMZIFs was uniformly coated on surfaces of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ under an optimized condition, the ideal precursor (MoO3 $@$ BMZIFs) with a core-shell structure was successfully prepared. During the subsequent pyrolysis process, the external BMZIFs spontaneously converted to porous carbons with certain transition metal/metal oxides and nitrogen dopings. Meanwhile, the internal $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ could either turn into $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ through a reduction reaction (Equation 1) or further react with carbon to form $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ by means of elevating the calcination temperature (Equation 2),40 achieving the facile transformation from metal oxide to carbide. Furthermore, it was found that an unexpected hollow structure could also be obtained in this system, which also showed great and impressive advantages for improving the lithium storage as the anode material.
$$
\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\mathrm{+C}\rightarrow\mathrm{MoO}_{2}+\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{x}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{MoO}_{2}\mathrm{+C}\to\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{x}}
$$
