The $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods precursor with a rectangle-like cross section was prepared through a facile hydrothermal method.41 The XRD pattern (Figure S1a) matches well with the simulated $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ phase (JCPDS No. 89-5108), confirming the formation of high purity $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ . The typical scanning electron microscope (SEM) and transmission scanning electron microscopy (TEM) images exhibit the obtained $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods have an average width around $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with a relatively smooth surface (Figure S1b and Figure 2a). To ensure the homogenous growth of BMZIFs on surfaces of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ , many attempts have been made and it was found that several factors such as the amount of reactants, reaction time and the introduction of additional surfactants could all play a vital role in growth of BMZIFs as shown in Figure S2. In this work, after mixing the PVP-modified $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods with zinc nitrate, cobalt nitrate and 2-MeIM successively in the methanol solution at an appropriate proportion and vigorously stirring for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ for the reaction, the one-dimensional $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\$ BMZIFs composite with rough surface was successfully prepared (Figure 2b). The TEM image in Figure 2c also confirms the uniform formation of BMZIFs on the surface of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ , forming an obvious core-shell structure with the thickness of BMZIFs shell around $150\mathrm{nm}$ measured from the high-magnification TEM image inset in Figure 2c. To further investigate the relationship between the structure evolution of the BMZIFs shell and the reaction time, Figure S3 exhibits morphologies of two derived samples with different reaction time. Although BMZIFs shell is successfully formed in each sample, the thickness and crystallinity of BMZIFs are lowered at a very short reaction time of $0.25\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure S3a). On the contrary, when the reaction time increases to 2h, the BMZIFs shell shows obvious overgrowth on the surface of the $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods, even some detached parts are clearly seen (Figure S3b), demonstrating a suitable reaction time of $0.5\mathrm{h}$ . The XRD result shown in Figure 2d also verifies the change of the BMZIFs shell during the reaction process as the intensity of peaks belonging to BMZIFs gets stronger with increase of the reaction time. Based on above-mentioned researches, the reaction mechanism about the homogeneous growth of BMZIFs on surfaces of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ is briefly illustrated in Figure 2e. As it illustrates, although the surface of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ is negatively charged, merely with the help of electrostatic forces, it still could not provide sufficient coordination sites to adsorb metal ions uniformly, which often results in incomplete and inhomogeneous growth of ZIFs. Nevertheless, when the surface of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ is enriched with PVP, amide carbonyl groups of PVP can fully coordinate with metal ions through chemical bonds and make it possible to achieve the uniform coating of ZIFs on surfaces of metal oxides under an optimized condition.42 More importantly, since the raw materials of MOFs are alterable and have a fast nucleation rate during the reaction, it is easy and controllable to get a layer of various MOFs coated on different substrate with a high yield in a short time. So this reported strategy that using MOFs as coating materials may shows promising prospects in structural design.
