In order to investigate the effect brought by the different carbonization temperatures on formation of Mo-based composites, many characterization methods were employed. As shown in Figure 3a, the XRD pattern of the sample obtained at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (denoted as $\mathrm{MoO_{2}/C-600}_{\mathrm{\it/}}$ ) shows several peaks indexed to the monoclinic $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ (JCPDS No. 76-1807), but the intensity is relatively low due to the formation of the outer thick carbon layer derived from BMZIFs during pyrolysis process, which reduces the degree of crystallinity of inner $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ . But after the acid washing, peaks located at $26.1^{\circ}$ and $37.1^{\circ}$ , belonging to the (-111) and (111) planes of $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ , respectively, become clear and the corresponding sample with a hollow structure is denoted as $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ (Figure S4a). Based on our experiments, it is found that $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ with a low degree of crystallinity could partially dissolved in the concentrated HCl to some extent, which results in the formation of hollow structure. When the temperature is elevated to $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ， $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ appears (denoted as $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}700\$ ) with well-indexed diffraction peaks located at $34.5^{\circ}$ , $39.5^{\circ}$ and $52.2^{\circ}$ ，assigned to the (002), (211) and (221) planes of the orthorhombic $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ (JCPDS No. 77-0720) (Figure 3a). The purity and crystallinity of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ are improved with the increase of temperature, like the sample obtained at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (denoted as $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}800\$ showing strong diffraction peaks in the XRD pattern (Figure S4b). Notably, during the pyrolysis process, except for the formation of porous carbons, there are to some extent metal oxides $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ and CoO) remained in all samples just as indicated in XRD results because of the carbonization of the Zn, Co-BMZIFs shell, which can also serve as active materials for LIBs to improve the reversible capacity. The Raman spectra clearly show D- and G-peaks belonging to carbon located at about 1350 and $1595~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 3b), respectively, demonstrating the existence of the free carbon derived from BMZIFs, and the $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ results of different samples are all close to 1.00 with very little difference, which indicates the amorphous state of the free carbon and will be further verified by the following TEM analysis. To further confirm the composition of different product,
Table S1 displays the contents of carbon, nitrogen as well as various metal elements in these four samples determined by the CHN elemental analysis and ICP-AES measurement. It is easily found that the Mo element is dominant compared with other metal elements like Zn and Co contained in all samples, and its proportion gets increased gradually with the increase of temperature, indicating the primary composition is Mo-based compound in all samples. Furthermore, XPS was also employed to study the surface chemical states and compositions of products obtained under different conditions. As shown in Figure S5, the respective full spectra identify the existence of Mo, Zn, Co, C, N and O in corresponding samples with distinguishing contents, and the high-resolution spectra of Mo 3d clearly exhibit the change from $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ . For two $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ samples obtained at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , each one has two peaks located at $232.4~\mathrm{eV}$ and $235.6\mathrm{eV}$ , which are ascribed to Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}$ in $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ ,43 respectively. Besides, the weak peak at 228.3 eV indicates trace amounts of ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{2+}$ .29 However, when $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ is transformed to $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ at a higher temperature, there are two peaks at $228.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $231.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ related to Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ of ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{2+}$ in $\mathsf{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ appearing and their intensities get stronger with the improved crystalline of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ . The other two peaks are assigned to $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}$ which may result from the partial oxidation of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ surface during the process of preservation or XPS measurements.44 Meanwhile, all samples have a relatively high nitrogen content derived from the pyrolysis of organic linkers in BMZIFs and each N 1s spectrum can be deconvoluted into three peaks at around 398.4, 399.1 and 400.5 eV, which correspond to pyridinic N, pyrrolic $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and graphitic N, respectively, confirming the existence of nitrogen in the carbon matrix.6,45 Plus, as indicated by the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (Figure 3c-f), A type-IV pattern with obvious hysteresis loop is clearly observed in each sample, verifying a mesoporous structure, and all samples have similar pore size distributions around $4\ \mathrm{nm}$ which is mainly contributed by the carbonization of the BMZIFs shell. By contrast, the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}800$ and $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ composites possess higher surface areas than $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ as the reaction between $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and C makes the pore structure more developed, while the $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ composites own the highest surface area due to the particular hollow structure. The BET surface areas of each sample are 33.4, 231.7, 105.5 and $153.6~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are mainly determined by their own microstructure that will be further verified in the following TEM analysis.
