Morphological and structural information of final products were further acquired by SEM and TEM. As shown in Figure 4a, b, the well-defined morphology of the MoO3@BMZIFs precursor is preserved in $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ composites after high-temperature calcination at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , remaining the former one-dimensional configuration with the thickness of carbon shell around $100~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ . And the corresponding SAED pattern with vague diffraction rings further demonstrates its poor crystallinity caused by the relatively thick carbon shell (inset in Figure 4c), which is consistent with XRD result. Seen from the high-resolution TEM image in Figure 4d, lattice fringes with a spacing of $0.34~\mathrm{nm}$ ascribed to the (-111) plane of monoclinic $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ are clearly observed, while the exterior carbon shell shows obvious porous and amorphous state as indicated in Figure 4e. The EDX result in Figure S6a reveals that the as-synthesized $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ composites mainly consist of C, N, O and Mo elements, and bits of residual Zn and Co elements derived from BMZIFs are also detected, Meanwhile, the element mapping also confirms proves the existence of all these elements with a uniform distribution (Figure S6b). Notably, after the MoO2/C-600 sample was treated with the concentrated HCl under the condition of the continuous stirring and heating, the obtained $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ composites could easily turn into a hollow nanotube structure as shown in Figure 4f, g. The carbon shell remains stable with a thickness around $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (inset in Figure ${\pmb4}\mathrm{g}$ ), but the inner $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ core is dissolved and transforms into numerous  small nanoparticles randomly embedded in the body of carbon matrix as marked in Figure 4h. The high-resolution TEM image gives clear lattice fringes with the measured spacing of 0.24 and $0.17{\mathrm{nm}}$ , assigned to the (111) and (-222) planes of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ with enhanced crystallinity (Figure 4h). Because of the acid washing, almost all metal oxides derived from BMZIFs are removed and the porous carbon in $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ composites thus reveals improved pore structure and degree of graphitization with trace of CoO nanoparticles remained as indicated in Figure 4j, which matches well with the previous XRD, Raman and BET results. Such a hollow structure of one-dimensional $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composites shows great potential as the anode material in boosting the lithium storage.
