When the carbonization temperature was elevated to $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ phase appeared. For $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ , the SEM and TEM images in Figure 5a,b confirm its one-dimensional core-shell structure. Due to the reaction between $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{~C~}$ to form $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ , the thickness of exterior carbon shell is reduced to ${\sim}70\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the interior core is composed of a great deal of assembled $\mathsf{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles instead of remaining intact like the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ core. The high-resolution TEM image in Figure 5c exhibits lattice fringes with a spacing of $0.22{\mathrm{nm}}$ which are ascribed to the (211) plane of orthorhombic $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ , and the corresponding FFT pattern of the marked area shows obvious diffraction spots, demonstrating the successful synthesis of $\mathsf{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ crystal. The microstructure of the carbon shell was also characterized by the high-resolution TEM image (Figure 5d), it displays a typical porous and amorphous feature with few CoO nanoparticles embedded, which is confirmed via the FFT pattern related to the marked area. However, when the temperature reached $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , taking the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}800$ sample as an example, it is found that the one-dimensional morphology and core-shell structure could be no longer well preserved owing to the excessive reaction and overwhelmed heat caused by too high temperature (Figure S7a), resulting in an inferior electrochemical performance when used as the anode material for LIBs (Figure S7b). Hence, the ideal temperature for the synthesis of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ based on the adopted strategy is chosen to be $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
