In view of their intriguing structural and compositional features, samples obtained at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were evaluated as anode materials for LIBs. To explain the lithium storage mechanism, the initial five cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the
MoO2/C-600, H-MoO2/C and $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ electrodes at $0.2\mathrm{~mV~s^{-1}~}$ in a voltage ranging from 0.01-3.0 V were shown in Figure 6a-c, respectively. For the MoO2/C-600 electrode (Figure 6a), an irreversible broad peak located around $0.7\mathrm{V}$ is exhibited in the first cathodic scan, which is attributed to the formation of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film owing to the electrolyte decomposition.46 And other several peaks located above $2\mathrm{~V~}$ which then disappear in the subsequent cycles correspond to the irreversible insertion of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ into superfine pores in the carbon matrix or the occurrence of side reactions caused by the residual meal oxides from BMZIFs such as CoO on the electrode surface.9,47,48 These factors usually result in a capacity loss in the first discharge-charge process. Besides, a reversible reduction peak shifting from $1.63\mathrm{~V~}$ to $1.17~\mathrm{V}$ after first cycle is associated with the phase transformation from the monoclinic $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ to the orthorhombic phase of the partially lithiated $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{MoO}_{2}+\mathrm{xLi}^{+}+\mathrm{xe}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{2})$ ,12,29 and the pronounced oxide peak at $1.48{\mathrm{~V~}}$ in the anodic scan is observed due to the delithiation of $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{Li_{x}M o O_{2}\rightarrow M o O_{2}+x L i^{+}+x e^{-}})$ . Meanwhile, the conversation mechanism of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ usually occurs at a relatively low potential when the size is in nanoscale.25 So as the CV cycle proceeds, the peaks which gradually appear at around $0.15\mathrm{~V~}$ can be attributed to the conversation reaction of $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}(\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{2}+(4-\mathrm{x})\mathrm{Li}^{+}+(4$ $-\mathrm{\boldmath~x)e^{-}\rightarrow\ M o+2L i_{2}O)}$ ,49 contributing to the additional capacity. The well-overlapped CV curves after the first cycle imply a stable and reversible discharge-charge process of the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ electrode. Similarly, although the formation of SEI film and the irreversible insertion of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ are inevitable in the first cycle, two pairs of redox peaks located at around 1.13/1.51 V and 1.62/1.77 V are shown in the initial five cycles of the $_\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ electrode (Figure 6b), also confirming the reversible phase transitions of $\mathrm{Li_{x}M o O_{2}}(0<\mathbf{x}\leq0.98)$ from monoclinic to orthorhombic.50 It is notable that the anodic current rises gradually in the region above $2\mathrm{V}$ after the activation process, the explanation for this phenomenon is generally that the catalytic activity of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ gets greatly improved after cycling, which then induces the decomposition of the polymeric SEI film and thereby contributes to the increase of the reversible capacity.51-53 When $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ serves as the electrode material, the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ electrode display different CV curves compared with the above-mentioned $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ electrodes. As shown in Figure 6c, after the initial activated process in the first cycle, there is a pair of obvious redox peaks located around $1.26/1.47\mathrm{~V~}$ appearing on the subsequent CV curves, which corresponds to the reversible conversation reaction between $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ and ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ $(\mathrm{Mo_{2}C\ +\ x L i^{+}+\ x e^{-}\ \rightarrow\ 2M o\ +\ L i_{x}C)}$ .19,22 This result demonstrates that the synthesized $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ in the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ electrode can serve as the main active material for lithium storage. Besides, there is no obvious redox peaks related to the reaction with CoO appearing above $2\mathrm{~V~}_{:}$ , eliminating the capacity contribution from the residual CoO. Figure S8 exhibits the discharge-charge curves of these three samples at a current density of 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . In the first cycle, $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ delivers the corresponding discharge and charge capacities of 1673 and 1096 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, with a low initial coulombic efficiency (CE) about $65\%$ (Figure S8a). The huge capacity loss in the first cycle is mainly ascribed to the occurrence of several irreversible side reactions, such as the formation of SEI film, the decomposition of the electrolyte molecules and the irreversible trapping of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ into the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ lattice as well as disordered carbon.29 The CE can quickly recover to $94\%$ in the second cycle with a high reversible capacity up to 1142 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and maintain stable (above $95\%$ ) in the following cycles. A pair of pronounced plateaus at about 1.21/1.45 V is observed in discharge-charge profiles after initial activation, which matches well with CV results. The $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ electrode in Figure S8b also show a high initial discharge capacity reaching to $1652\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with a low CE around $66\%$ . Such unsatisfactory initial coulombic efficiency has always been an issue for metal oxides electrode materials. Notably, the reversible capacity of $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ gradually increases upon cycling and this phenomenon is commonly referred in other reported Mo-based compounds.12,54 Based on the previous studies, the capacity increase is mainly attributed to the following reasons. One is that the lithiation mechanism of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ spontaneously changes from the intercalation to the conversation reaction upon cycling,55 which promises more $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ ( $0<\mathbf{x}\leq0.98)$ translates into Mo and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , thus contributing to the extra capacity. The other reason is about the reversible decomposition of polymeric SEI film,52,56 which gets enhanced by the newly generated $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanodomain with high catalytic activity after the electrode activation process. For $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ , as exhibited in Figure S8c, it displays lower discharge and charge capacities of 1219 and $810\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ compared with $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ composites in the first cycle, respectively. Likewise, the initial capacity loss is closely related to the formation of SEI film and the irreversible trapping of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ in $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{C}$ . However, after that, the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode maintains high reversible discharge and charge capacities with stable CE up to $98\%$ in the subsequent cycles, showing great potential in cycling stability.
densities ranging from 0.1 to $2{\mathrm{~A~g}}^{-1}$ . Each sample shows a good rate capability. At the current density of 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the corresponding discharge capacities of MoO2/C-600, $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ and $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ are 1066, 1107 and $775\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at the $10^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle, respectively. And they can still retain to 473, 587 and $425\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ when the current density is increased to 2 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Besides, even the current density is recovered to $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ after 50 cycles, all these three samples exhibit the reversible capacities of 1008, 1057 and $768\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, well implying their own stable rate performances. In comparison, the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ electrode displays the best rate capability with the slightest capacity decay between various current densities and $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ shows superior rate performance than $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ . To further evaluate the cycling performance of the electrode material at a high current density, the obtained final products were tested at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for more than 600 cycles. As presented in Figure 6e, the reversible capacity of $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ drops a great deal in the initial 40 cycles caused by many irreversible side reactions like the formation of SEI film and the irreversible trapping of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ into the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ lattice or superfine pores of the disordered carbon.29,46 But it then gradually increase to more than $780\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles, which is generally attributed to the reversible growth of a polymeric gel-like film resulting from kinetically activated electrolyte degradation with the increase of cycling times.40,51 This common phenomenon, in which the capacity goes down to some extent and then gradually rises up, has been widely observed in transition metal oxide based anode materials for LIBs and more detailed researches about the electrochemical reaction process are still needed.57,58 After 700 cycles, the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ electrode can still deliver a stable discharge capacity up to $702\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with a high coulombic efficiency around $99\%$ . Similarly, after acid washing, the obtained $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ electrode also exhibits an excellent long-term cycling performance (Figure 6f). It shows a fewer capacity loss in initial cycles and has a higher reversible capacity increased to $810\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 600 cycles compared with $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ , which is benefited from the designed hollow structure and the reduced size of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanoparticles that are well wrapped by porous carbons. Although the specific capacity of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ is inferior to $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode displays a more stable cycling performance compared with the mentioned two kinds of $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ electrodes, showing a reversible capacity up to $528\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ even after 600 cycles, which is much higher than the commercial graphite anode material (372 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in theory) and pure $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ crystals with low reaction activity $(240\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ as exhibited in Figure S7b). Therefore, all optimized products derived from the $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\$ BMZIFs precursor exhibit superior electrochemical performances as anode materials for LIBs, even exceed most reported Mo-based composites (Table S2). And these results fully indicate the well-designed $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@$ BMZIFs nanorods have the great potential in obstructing carbon coated Mo-based composites with enhanced lithium storage through a controllable way.
