As an effective method to gain insights into the electrochemistry, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is adopted to examine the interfacial process and kinetics of electrodes. Figure 7a displays the Nyquist plots of three synthesized composites before cycle. Each plot consists of an obvious semicircle in the high frequency followed by a small depressed semicircle, which are related to the charge-transfer resistance in the electrolyte-electrode interface $(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{CT}})$ and the effect caused by the formation of the SEI film $(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{SEI}})$ , respectively, and a straight line in low frequency associated with the ion diffusion in the electrode material $(Z_{\mathrm{W}})$ is also included. An equivalent electric circuit is present inset in Figure 7a. In comparison, the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ electrode shows the lowest charge-transfer resistance due to the introduction of the conductive nitrogen-doped carbon shell from BMZIFs,45 promoting electrons transfer from the inner $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ composition. And because of the good metal conductivity of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ , the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode also exhibits a low charge-transfer resistance. Although the charge-transfer resistance of the $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ electrode is relatively high, especially the part caused by the SEI film, a steep line is observed in low frequency meaning the fast ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ diffusion in the electrode material, which thanks to the advantageous hollow porous structure with a high surface area, thus promising the sufficient immersion of active materials in the electrolyte and shortening the pathways for ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ diffusion.32. Figure 7b shows their own Nyquist plots after 50 cycles. It is obviously found that the diameter of the semicircle corresponding to each sample is greatly reduced after the activation process, which may be attributed to the more complete conversion reaction with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ and the stabilization of the SEI film with the increase of cycling times.24 The $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ electrode possesses the smallest semicircle and the steepest line compared with the other two samples, showing the lowest charge-transfer resistance and fastest ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ diffusion in electrode, which contributes to the increase of the reversible capacity upon cycling. The EIS results displayed in Figure 7b are well consistent with the previously discussed cycling performances of different samples. In addition, to demonstrate the structural stability of each sample during battery cycling, these three electrodes after 50 charge and discharge cycles were carefully recovered and re-examined by TEM images. As shown in the Figure S9, although the surface of electrodes seems mussier and rougher compared with those before cycling, which mainly results from the continuous insertion and extraction of lithium ions, the integral nanostructures of all samples remain relatively well with clear 1D morphologies instead of breaking into pieces, indicating a substantial stability of our products. Among them, the $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ electrode shows the best result after cycles, which strongly proves its superior cycling performance.
