To further understand the outstanding electrochemical performance, especially the good rate capability of $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}$ composites, CV measurement was adopted to investigate electrochemical kinetics of the designed electrode. Figure 8a displays the
CV profiles of the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode at various scan rates ranging from 0.2 to 1.5 $\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}$ . It is notable that the measured current $(i)$ is not proportional to the square root of the san rate $(\nu)$ at a certain potential, which indicates the redox process is not a simple ion-diffusion control process.59,60 In general, the interrelation between the current $(i)$ and the scan rate $(\nu)$ can be calculated by the equation:
$$
\scriptstyle{i=a\nu}^{b}
$$
where $a$ and $b$ are empirical values. According to previous researches, the $b$ -value of 0.5 represents a diffusion-controlled process, while $b$ -value of 1 means the ideal capacitive behavior.17 As shown in Figure 8b, by plotting the fitting lines of log $(i)$ and log $(\nu)$ at different potentials of the reduction process, a series of $b$ values larger than 0.8 are obtained, indicating a high capacitive contribution for the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode. The total capacitive contribution at a fixed scan rate can be quantitatively analyzed by distinguishing the fraction of capacitor-like $(k_{1}\nu)$ and diffusion-controlled $(k_{2}\nu^{1/2})$ currents at a fixed potential $(V)$ according to the following equation:61
$$
i(V){=}k_{1}{\nu}{+}k_{2}{\nu}^{1/2}
$$
By changing equation (2) to
$$
i(V)/\nu^{1/2}{=}k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
the $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ values can be easily calculated via plotting the fitting lines of $i(V)/\nu^{1/2}$ and $\nu^{1/2}$ . And then the capacitive current $i_{\mathrm{c}}(V)=k_{\mathrm{l}}\nu$ could be distinguished from the total measured current with the value of $k_{2}$ . As exhibited in Figure 8c, it is found that nearly $80\%$ fraction of the total current in the reduction process comes from capacitive contribution at a scan rate of $\boldsymbol{1}\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}{-}700$ electrode, and the capacitive contribution shows a trend of rising with increased scan rates (Figure 8d). The distinct capacitive behavior is usually believed to originate from the reduced particle size of active materials and the highly conductive matrix, which has been demonstrated in previous reports.62,63 In contrast, the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composites obtained under the same condition in this work possess an inferior capacitive effect compared with $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ . Taking the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ sample as an example (Figure S10), the $b$ values of the prepared $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ electrode are relatively low and the capacitive contribution is only $43.6\%$ at a scan rate of $1~\mathrm{mV~\s^{-1}}$ , revealing the obvious diffusion-controlled process for the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ electrode when reacting with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ . So the high capacitive contribution of the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode plays an important role in enhancing its own electrochemical performance. Due to the capacitive effect, a great deal of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ are inclined to be stored on the surface or in the near-surface area of the electrode,60 which could greatly avoid the structural damage of the active material and result in excellent cycling stability. Meanwhile, several advantages brought by the capacitive behavior such as the rapid electron transfer and the short ion diffusion length also contribute to the high-rate capability of the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ electrode.
Hence, based on the above-mentioned analyses, the outstanding electrochemical performances of the well-designed Mo-based composites, including the superior specific capacity, rate capability and cycling stability, can be ascribed to the favorable synergetic effect of the following factors. First, the one-dimensional core-shell or hollow structure as well as nanostructured design of active materials can not only afford more active sites for lithium storage, but also shorten the ion diffusion distance and direct current pathways to significantly accelerate reaction kinetics.45 Second, the outer nitrogen-enriched porous carbon shell derived from Zn, Co-BMZIFs can play a crucial role in enhancing the electrical conductivity of the electrode material and buffering the volume change effectively during cycling. Furthermore, the capacitive effect also makes great contributions to the enhancement of the electrochemical performance as previously discussed.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, a series of one-dimensional Mo-based/carbon composites with distinct nanostructures have been controllably prepared through a metal-organic frameworks mediated method. In this process, the successful fabrication of the core-shell $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\$ BMZIFs nanorods is the key step, as the BMZIFs shell can serve as both carbon sources and the reactant to react with $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ simultaneously during carbonization, maintaining the structural stability of pyrolytic products. When applied as anode materials for LIBs, all final products with optimized nanostructures, including MoO2/C-600, $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ , show great improvements in aspects of the reversible specific capacity, rate capability and long-term cycling stability. Such superior electrochemical performances are mainly contributed by many beneficial factors like the stable nanostructure, good conductivity and improved reaction kinetics, which are greatly enhanced by the one-dimensional nanostructured design, N-doped carbon coating and productive capacitance contribution. Therefore, this reported work can not only enlighten the application of MOFs as coating materials but also boost the development of Mo-based compounds for energy storage and utilization.
## METHODS
## Material Synthesis
Preparation of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods: $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods were synthesized according to a reported method with some modifications.41 In brief, $1.4\mathrm{~g~}(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{6}\mathrm{Mo_{7}O_{24}}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ was dissolved in a $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of mixed solution consisting of $65\%$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ and deionized $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with a volume ratio of 1:5. After ultrasonic dispersion for $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the solution was transferred into a Teflon autoclave and heated at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that, the product was obtained by centrifugation and washed with ethanol for several times before drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
Preparation of the $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\$ BMZIFs precursor: In a typical procedure, $\mathbf{0.3}\mathrm{~g~}$ of as-prepared $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods were fully grinded and then dispersed into $90~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol containing $1\mathrm{g}$ of PVP (K-30). After ultrasonic dispersion and vigorous agitation, $2.125\ \mathrm{g}$ of $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.104\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ (the molar ratio of $Z{\mathrm n}^{2+}/{\mathrm C}{0}^{2+}$ was 20) were subsequently dissolved into the mixed solution and stirred for at least $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, $4.926\ \mathrm{g}$ of 2-methylimidazole dissolved in $90~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol was slowly added into the above solution followed by vigorous stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\$ BMZIFs precursor was collected by centrifugation, washed with methanol for several times and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
Preparation of Mo-based/C composites: In a typical procedure, the as-synthesized $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}@\$ BMZIFs was annealed at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a ramp rate of $5~\mathrm{{^{o}C}~m i n^{-1}}$ to obtain $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composites denoted as $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}{-}600$ . After washing by the concentrated HCl under the condition of the continuous stirring and heating, the hollow structure was generated and the sample is denoted as $_{\mathrm{H-MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}}$ . To prepare $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}$ composites, the carbonization temperature was increased above $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the corresponding products are denoted as $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}700$ and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}{-}800\$ , respectively.
## Material Characterizations
The prepared samples were characterized by X-ray diffraction (X'Pert PRO MPD, Holland, Cu Kα $\lambda{=}1.518$ Å), Raman spectrometer (Jobin-Yvon Labram-010), elemental analyses (Vario EL CUBE) coupled with coupled plasma-atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (PHI 5000 VersaProbe, ULVAC-PHI, Japan), $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption analysis (Micromeritics ASAP2020), field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) (Hitachi S-4800, Japan) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (JEM- 2100UHR, Japan).
## Electrochemical Measurements
The working electrode was fabricated by mixing $80\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ active materials, $10\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ carbon black and 10 wt% polyvinilidene difluoride (PVDF) in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone to form a homogenous slurry. Then the obtained slurry was coated on a piece of copper foil uniformly and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ . The mass loading of each electrode was around $1.2~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . CR2032 coin-type half cells were finally assembled in an Ar-filled glove box by using the prepared material as working electrode, lithium foil as the counter and reference electrode, polypropylene Celgard 2400 film as the separator, and $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ dissolved in a 1:1 (v/v) ethylene carbonate and dimethyl carbonate (EC/DMC) as the electrolyte. The assembled half cells were galvanostatically discharge-charged between 0.01-3 V vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ on a Land CT2001A battery test system. Both electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were performed on Ametek PARSTAT4000 electrochemistry workstation. The EIS data were measured with a $5~\mathrm{mV}$ amplitude signal in a range of $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ and $0.05~\mathrm{Hz}$ in selected cycles, and CV curves were recorded at a series of scan rate ranging from $0.2{-}1.5~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathrm{\bfs}^{-1}$ within the voltage of 0.01-3 V. All electrochemical measurements were tested at ambient temperature.
